 "source_type": "instagram",
 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DfPnWQFM1SP/",
 "title": "Should you accept a counteroffer after resigning?",
 "topic": "mindset",
 "summary": "The creator claims that when you resign, companies often suddenly find the budget for a salary hike and treat you as important. However, he warns that a counteroffer is often just an immediate need to retain you, and you must evaluate if it truly solves the real reasons you wanted to leave.",
 "key_points": [
  "Ask yourself why you are leaving: is it only about money, or is it culture, management, or lack of growth?",
  "A salary hike might solve the money problem, but it will not automatically solve everything else.",
  "The creator claims counteroffers are often about the company's immediate need to retain you due to project delivery or client reliance, rather than recognizing your value.",
  "Do not just evaluate the money they offer; evaluate the future you are choosing to stay for.",
  "The creator says you should not reject every counteroffer automatically.",
  "If the company genuinely addresses your concerns, gives you a better role, and puts commitments in writing, it is worth evaluating.",
  "Do not let a sudden salary hike make you forget the reason you wanted to leave."
 ],
 "content": "ep: Should you accept COUNTEROFFER after resigning? if your company suddenly offers you a salary hike after you put down your resignation think twice before accepting it because here's something you need to understand: For the last 2 years, you have been asking for an increment. Every time the answer was the same. There's no budget right now. Let's say in next appraisal cycle. But the moment you submit your resignation, suddenly there's a meeting with your manager. The budget is available, the hike is possible and suddenly you are the one of the most important people in the team. Now ask yourself one question. If the company could offer you this hike today, why couldn't they do it when you were asking for it? Sometimes a counteroffer isn't necessarily about recognizing your value. It's about the company's immediate need to retain you. Maybe your replacement will take time, a project is in a middle of delivery or the client depends heavily on you. But the bigger question is: Why did you resign in the first place? Was it only about salary or was it your manager, work culture, lack of growth or the opportunities you weren't getting? Because a salary hike might solve the problem, but it won't automatically solve everything else. I'm not saying you should reject every counteroffer. If the company genuinely address your concerns, give you better role, puts the commitment in writing, it's worth evaluating. But don't let a sudden salary hike make you forget the reason you wanted to leave. Remember, don't just evaluate the money they are offering you, evaluate the future you are choosing to stay for. Hope you understand. Till then don't forget to follow me, see you in next one.",
